  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 231 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: As upper level flow remains northwesterly over \nour region under an upper level trough we will have continued \nchances for isolated rain showers this afternoon and evening and\nagain on Saturday. Surface high pressure will ridge into our \narea from the north on Sunday and into Monday, leading to drier \nweather during that period. Will have areas of fog each \novernight, highs generally in the 70s areawide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The more dynamic fall-like synoptic pattern is \nbeginning to become established. Going into mid and late week, a\ndeepening low will move east from Ontario into Quebec, with \nensemble guidance indicating it will likely drop blow 990 mb by \nthe time it reaches Atlantic Canada. The center of the low \nshould pass by well to the north and the best dynamics should \nmiss the region. While warm air advection will occur mid-week \nand bring a more humid airmass into the region, there will not \nbe enough time for a fully tropical airmass to become \nestablished. Therefore, this will limit instability as the \nsystem passes by. During Wednesday night and into the day on \nThursday, multiple boundaries/troughs/fronts will pass through \nand bring multiple rounds of showers. A favored nocturnal \npassage of the strongest dynamics and a marginal airmass should \nprevent a widespread severe threat, but with such a strong low \nto the north the threat will still be watched. Afterward, the \ncoolest airmass of the season so far will become established, \nwith widespread highs in the 60s (and potentially 50s in the \ncooler spots) for Friday. Depending on the position of the \nsurface high over the weekend, first frost could be possible in \nthe cold hollows.",
